@* Written in each component, not in an _Imports.razor, whose class Razor makes public (ADR-0201).
   Every product using is written with global::. The component lives in ExPivot.Components, where a
   plain "ExPivot.Engine" could be looked up against the ExPivot component class first, and a plain
   "ExGrid.X" against sibling types (the CS0426 trap in AGENTS.md). The Web namespace makes @on...
   handlers compile as event handlers rather than as plain HTML attributes. *@
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using global::ExGrid
@using global::ExGrid.Cells
@using global::ExGrid.Chrome
@using global::ExGrid.Columns
@using global::ExGrid.Components
@using global::ExGrid.Rows
@using global::ExGrid.Selection
@using global::ExPivot.Chrome
@using global::ExPivot.Engine
@* Not the contract (ADR-0201): ExPivot renders this inside its dialog frame as Show Details'
   dialog when the Pivot Chrome draws none, and no Consumer places it. It is public only because
   Razor makes every component public, is hidden from a Consumer's editor, and may change in any
   version. *@
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
@* The built-in content of Show Details' dialog (ADR-0059), inside ExPivot's frame: the cell's
   title, its records, and Close, which takes the keyboard when the dialog opens. *@
<div class="ex-pivot-dialog-content">
    <div class="ex-pivot-dialog-title">@Context.Title</div>
    <div class="ex-pivot-dialog-records">@Context.Records</div>
    <div class="ex-pivot-panel-actions">
        <PivotFocusButton Class="ex-pivot-close" OnClick="Context.Close" FocusRequest="Context.FocusRequest" TakeKeyboard="Context.TakeKeyboard">@Context.Word("close")</PivotFocusButton>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The title, the records and the way out (ADR-0059).</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public PivotDetailsDialogContext Context { get; set; } = default!;
}
